A||||5||Eric||Clutch line install
Z||000000||Eric||05-20-99||03:27 PM||||I just got my new clutch line from the Fiero Store today.  The line is almost exactly the same as the original.  It has a braided flex line on the end that attaches to the slave and a flex line that lies just under the front trunk.  There is a mess of bends and twists on the master cylinder end and I am worried, even with the flex hose aiding me, I won't be able to squeeze the hard line and fitting through the brake/clutch line opening.  There is also a rubber spacer, where the lines pass through the opening, that is in my way and I worried I will rip it if I take it out.  I hope someone out there has done this before and can help me through it.  Thanks.
Z||000001||Eric||05-20-99||04:58 PM||||This is killing me!  I took out the piece of plastic under the spare tire and the rubber spacer that holds the lines apart and I still can't get the line routed all the to the master cylinder.  Oh well, back to work.
Z||000002||Eric||05-20-99||09:53 PM||||Well, it's on.  Took awhile but I think the investment in the new line will pay off.  My best guess as to what happened is the rubber inside the braided hoses deteriorated to the point where it was letting air into the line and just a little bit of fluid was leaking out the rear hose.  Don't rush out and buy a new line yet.  I'm going to test mine out for a few weeks before I determine what the problem was.  I did, however, replace the master, slave, and now the line, so that makes the whole hydralic system new. 
Z||000003||Patrick||05-21-99||01:29 PM||||Hi Eric,<p>There are many of us who have problems with the hydraulic clutch systems in our Fieros. I'm sure in a lot of situations that the rubber part of the line is allowing air in and we're blaming the slave cylinder, etc. instead. <p>When you decided to change the line, did you have the option to change just the rubber portions of the line? Or is that impossible?<p>
Z||000004||Eric||05-21-99||02:46 PM||||I tried to change just the rubber line in the first place, after I changed the master and slave cylinder.  The problem is the fittings and line are metric and unless you have access to the flaring tools new fitting and a few other odd little things, you won't be able to make the new line seal completly to the old hard line.  It took me about three weeks and about $75 to figure this out for myself.  There is also a second flex line that lies just under the spare tire shelf.  I didn't realize this and may have had these problems continue if I had not bought a whole new line.  The Fiero Store still had a few of these in stock when I ordered mine.  Putting the new line in is not easy.  There is some bending involved, but new fitting are included.  <p>I just tested my car out a few minutes ago for the first time since I swapped lines.  I could tell I wasn't gaining air or losing pressure.  The pedal felt consistant.  However, I do think I need to bleed it again, but this is normal after completly draining the fluid.  I guess time will tell.<p>I have some problems with my shifter also.  The car doesn't always want to go into first and tends to pop out of third.  I think I just need to adjust the cables, but I am not sure how to do this.  All other gears are fine, including reverse.  Need a new shift knob too.  The old one's threads were worn and it didn't come off the shifter this time, so I gave it a good choppin with a saw.  It looks kind of silly now.<p>Patrick, I can't remember for sure, but did you think up the trick of putting two pistons into one slave cylinder?  I tried it for myself and discovered the clutch would not engage on my V6 five speed.  Maybe you have a different configuration and it works for you, but it sure doesn't work for me.
Z||000005||Patrick||05-21-99||03:15 PM||||<br>No Eric, that two piston trick is Paul's. (If it HAD worked for you, I would've taken the credit though. <IMG SRC="http://forums.gagames.com/forums/smile.gif"> )<p>I'll be following your progress in the forum. Good luck.<p> 
